WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.388
Each of these dangers has a guard in
the code, not a reminder in a runbook.

2
00:00:04.388 --> 00:00:09.173
The most dangerous is the quiet one: an
update that forgets a restriction and

3
00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:14.338
switches it off, so the writer re-reads the
live policy and refuses anything looser.

4
00:00:14.338 --> 00:00:18.476
A retroactive start date breaks
rotations on the very day they're due.

5
00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:22.212
A certificate that replaces the
old one takes the workload down.

6
00:00:22.212 --> 00:00:26.788
And an unattended app that can update
every application is a standing risk,

7
00:00:26.788 --> 00:00:29.150
so writes are always a signed-in person.

8
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.520
Notice the pattern: each
guard runs every time.
